// Compiler/TargetFacilities.maxon — WHICH HOST FACILITY A RUNTIME ENTRY NEEDS, AND WHICH TARGETS HAVE IT.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS FILE REPLACES A QUESTION THAT COULD NOT BE ASKED: `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`.** That
// predicate took a callee and answered a bool, and the bool meant *"x64-windows only"* — so the moment ONE
// family landed on a SECOND target the name became a lie and the answer became wrong for every other
// family at once. There was no seam to land a family through: the gate said `not
// targetHasWin32Substrate(target)` and refused everything the roster named, on every lane but one.
//
// The question it could not ask is the one a port actually poses: **does THIS TARGET provide the facility
// THIS CALLEE needs?** Two independent facts — a callee's requirement and a target's capability — which the
// old shape had fused into one. They are separated here:
//
//   • `calleeHostFacility(callee)` — the callee's requirement, and the door to E3104. One `HostFacility` or
//     `none`; the per-family predicates it consults still live with their runtimes, so a band added to one
//     of them is still gated by construction rather than by memory.
//   • `targetProvidesFacility(target, facility)` — the target's capability. The table a BACKEND writes into
//     when it lands a family, and the ONE place a lane's progress is recorded.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO ARE NOT IN LOCKSTEP, AND ONE ROW PROVES IT: A FACILITY MAY BE IN THE TABLE AND NOT IN
// `calleeHostFacility`.** `terminalDetection` is exactly that — its lane row is read by
// `TerminalRuntime.installTerminalRuntime` to pick a BODY rather than by this file to refuse a CALL, because
// the absence of terminal detection is an ANSWER ("not a terminal") where the absence of a file surface is
// not. See that enum case for the whole argument and for what it cost to learn. ⇒ A new facility must
// decide which of the two shapes it is; being in the enum does not by itself make it a refusal.
//
// ⚠ **THE TABLE IS THE BACKEND'S CLAIM AND MUST BE KEPT HONEST BY THE BACKEND.** A `true` here promises that
// the target's isel lowers every `StdOp` the facility's runtime bodies emit. Write one before the lowering
// exists and the E3104 stops firing while the backend still panics three tiers down — which is exactly the
// failure `SemanticCheck.requireTargetSupportsCallee`'s header records for the socket runtime, one level up.
// The backends' own `panic` arms are the backstop for that mistake, never the user-facing rejection.
//
// ⚠ **A FACILITY IS NAMED FOR WHAT THE HOST PROVIDES, NOT FOR THE API THAT PROVIDES IT.** `wallClock` rather
// than `getSystemTimeAsFileTime`: two platforms answer the same question through different calls, and a
// facility named after one of them cannot be claimed by the other without reading as a lie.

// ⭐ THE HOST FACILITIES A MAXON PROGRAM CAN REACH — one case per band whose runtime bodies bottom out in
// `StdOp`s a backend must lower with real OS calls, i.e. per family whose absence a program can NOTICE.
//
// ⚠ **THIS LINE READ "one case per family whose absence is a REFUSAL", AND `terminalDetection` FALSIFIED
// IT.** For every other case here the absence leaves a program with no answer at all, and the honest
// response is E3104 at the call's own span. For that one the absence IS an answer, so its row picks a
// runtime BODY instead. What every case still shares is the sentence above: a `true` promises a lowering.
//
// ⚠ Deliberately NOT a case per runtime ENTRY POINT. `__gt_now_ns` and `__Builtins.currentTimeMs` are one
// facility asked twice, and a target that lowers the monotonic clock lowers both or neither — so splitting
// them would invite a table with two answers to one question.
// ⚠ EXPORTED, and a `module`/file-private narrowing will be REVERSED by the build: `SemanticCheck`
// reads this enum for the services gate (`requireTargetSupportsServiceEntry`), which asks this table
// rather than refusing blind. An unused-export sweep narrowed it once, after which that gate stopped
// compiling.
export enum HostFacility
	// The scheduler's monotonic counter (`osReadClock`) — `__gt_now_ns` and every `Clock.*` above it.
	monotonicClock
	// The calendar (`osReadWallClock`) — `__clock_now_unix_s`. Split from the monotonic clock because a
	// reading that steps backwards when NTP corrects a drift is useless to a deadline, which is the same
	// split `ClockRuntime`'s header makes for the same reason.
	wallClock
	// This thread's own CPU consumption (`osThreadCpuTicks`) — `__thread_cpu_ticks`. Its own facility
	// because its UNIT is platform-defined (TSC ticks vs nanoseconds) and its API is unrelated to either
	// clock above: a target can serve both wall and monotonic time and still have no per-thread cost clock,
	// which is exactly WASI's position.
	threadCpuClock
	// How long the MACHINE has been up (`osTickCountMs`) — `__uptime_ms`. Its own facility rather than a
	// corner of `wallClock`, on this enum's stated rule that a facility is named for what the HOST provides:
	// a monotonic count since boot and a calendar are different questions with different APIs, and the
	// answer to one says nothing about the other.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OP BEHIND IT LOWERS ON MORE LANES THAN THIS ROW CLAIMS, AND THE ROW IS STILL HONEST.** Every
	// POSIX lane has a `mrt_host_tick_ms` chunk, but each installs it only under `usesIocp` — so the
	// callee's `iatCall`/`callDirect` would relocate against a chunk that is not in the image for a program
	// that reads the uptime and spawns nothing. A lane claims this row by widening that install gate, which
	// is the "a `true` promises a lowering" rule this file's header states.
	uptimeClock
	// The timed park (`osSleepMs`) — `__gt_sleep`, and so `sleep(ms)` through `stdlib/Sleep.maxon`.
	sleep
	// The cooperative reschedule (`__gt_resched`) — `Runtime.yield()`. The scheduler's run-queue handoff
	// through `__gt_context_switch`, which is a hand-assembled per-CPU chunk rather than a lowering.
	reschedule
	// The green-thread substrate a `select` drives (`__gt_await_any`), and the whole `async`/`await` floor
	// under it.
	greenThreads
	// A child process — spawn, its three pipes, and the wait (`__gt_subp_*`, `__gt_process_run`,
	// `__gt_io_read`).
	subprocess
	// The file surface (`__mf_*`) — open, read, write, close, size, delete, rename, stat.
	managedFile
	// The directory surface (`__md_*`) — exists, create, current path, and the search walk.
	managedDirectory
	// The socket surface (`__sock_*`).
	managedSocket
	// The program's own argument vector (`__cl_*`). Its own facility rather than a corner of `processInfo`
	// because argv has a DIFFERENT SHAPE per platform — one unsplit string on Windows, a pre-split vector
	// under SysV — so a target serves it as a rung rather than as a lowering.
	commandLine
	// Standard input (`__con_*`).
	consoleStdin
	// The process's own IDENTITY: its executable path and its pid (`__proc_exe_path`, `__proc_pid`).
	processInfo
	// The process's own SCHEDULING PRIORITY (`__proc_bg_priority`). Its own facility rather than a corner of
	// `processInfo` because the two are a DIFFERENT KIND of question and the platforms split them that way:
	// reading an identity is one call that cannot fail, while lowering a priority is a WRITE whose SCOPE
	// disagrees across POSIX — `setpriority(PRIO_PROCESS, ...)` scopes to the process on Darwin and to the
	// calling THREAD on Linux, so on a Linux lane the two verbs are not the same verb at all
	// (`StdToArm64Conversion.lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriority` carries the argument, and the arm64-linux row in
	// `targetProvidesFacility` carries what that scope difference still leaves owed now that the lane has
	// worker Ms).
	//
	// ⚠ **THIS PARAGRAPH ENDED "arm64-linux serves the identity half today and not the priority half, which is
	// exactly the PARTIAL LANDING this file was built to make expressible", AND L4 CLOSED THAT PARTIAL
	// LANDING.** The split earns its keep anyway, and for a stronger reason than the schedule it was written
	// about: `processInfo` landed a whole rung EARLIER than `processPriority` on that lane precisely because
	// they are separate rows. What the split records now is the DIFFERENCE IN KIND, which no landing changes —
	// reading an identity cannot fail, lowering a priority is a write whose scope disagrees across POSIX.
	processPriority
	// How many processors the machine has (`__cpu_count`).
	cpuCount
	// Whether a standard stream is a TERMINAL (`__tty_*`). Its own facility rather than a corner of
	// `consoleStdin`, on this enum's stated rule that a facility is named for what the HOST provides:
	// reading standard input and asking the OS what KIND of object a handle is are two different questions
	// with two different APIs, and a lane can serve either without the other — Win32 answers the second with
	// `GetFileType` and POSIX with `isatty`, neither of which has anything to do with the read.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE ROW HERE THAT DOES NOT PRODUCE AN E3104, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE SHAPE OF THE
	// ABSENCE.** Every other facility's absence leaves a program with NO ANSWER — there is no honest `read
	// stdin` on a lane with no stdin — so `calleeHostFacility` maps those bands to a refusal at the call's
	// own span. The absence of terminal detection IS an answer: "this is not a terminal", the conservative
	// half, and what `--color=auto` resolved to everywhere before any of this existed. So this row is read
	// by `TerminalRuntime.installTerminalRuntime`, which emits the detecting body where it is `true` and a
	// `return 0` body where it is `false`, and the callee exists on every lane.
	//
	// ⛔ **THAT DISTINCTION WAS PAID FOR.** Mapped into `calleeHostFacility` like its neighbours, it made
	// shv2 UNBUILDABLE for arm64-macOS in a day: `Testing/TestRender` asks the question unconditionally,
	// `Testing/` is ordinary user code, and this gate is reachability-blind for user code by design.
	terminalDetection
	// A NAMED SHARED SECTION two processes both map (`__shm_*`). Its own facility rather than a corner of
	// `managedFile`, on this enum's stated rule that a facility is named for what the HOST provides:
	// publishing a section under a name and mapping it into another process is a different API from
	// reading a file, and a lane can serve either without the other.
	sharedMemory
end 'HostFacility'

// The answer `calleeHostFacility` returns. A union rather than a bool-plus-out-param so that "this callee
// needs nothing" is a case the caller must handle, not a sentinel it can forget to test.
union CalleeFacility
	needs(facility HostFacility)
	none
end 'CalleeFacility'

// ⭐ **WHICH HOST FACILITY THIS CALLEE NEEDS — the roster the E3104 gate reads.**
//
// Membership is `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`'s, unchanged, and its rule is unchanged with it:
// **a name joins when SOURCE names it and the parser routes it here.** A compiler-INSERTED `__gt_*` call
// (`__gt_promise_drop` at a scope exit, `__gt_io_park` inside an `__mf_*` body) is deliberately absent,
// because an E3104 against one of those names a runtime entry the user has never heard of at a span where
// they wrote nothing. `SemanticCheck.requireTargetSupportsCallee`'s header carries that argument in full,
// including the two `async-linearity` cases that measured it.
//
// ⚠ **THE ORDER OF THE TESTS IS NOT ARBITRARY WHERE TWO COULD MATCH.** They cannot today — every family
// predicate is a distinct prefix or an exact name — and the four individually-named scheduler entries are
// tested FIRST so that a prefix band added later cannot silently swallow one of them.
// ⭐⭐ **WHY EACH FAMILY IS ON THE ROSTER — PRESERVED VERBATIM FROM `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`, WHOSE
// MEMBERSHIP THIS IS.** Every paragraph below was written at the moment its family joined, and several
// record a MEASUREMENT that is not re-derivable from the code. The one framing that has changed is the
// conclusion: where these say "x64-windows only", read "needs a facility whose provider is named in
// `targetProvidesFacility`" — the reasons for MEMBERSHIP are untouched, because a family is on the roster
// for what it NEEDS, which no port changes.
//
// ⭐ **THE RUNTIME ENTRIES A SOURCE-WRITTEN BUILTIN LOWERS TO THAT ONLY A HOST FACILITY CAN PROVIDE.** The
// exact set `requireTargetSupportsCallee` refuses below (E3104), and the deliberate counterpart of
// `targetHasWin32Substrate` below it: one says what a CALLEE needs, the other what a TARGET has. It is
// also what `stdlibSubstrateEntries` ASKS to seed its fixpoint — that fixpoint propagates this answer
// through the stdlib call graph rather than keeping a second list of entries to drift from it.
//
//   • `__gt_now_ns` — `osReadClock`'s QueryPerformanceCounter/Frequency (`__Builtins.currentTimeNanos`,
//     `currentTimeMs`, and every `Clock.*` written against them);
//   • `__clock_*` — `osReadWallClock`'s GetSystemTimeAsFileTime (`__Builtins.currentUnixTimeSeconds`);
//   • `__gt_sleep` — the green-thread timer park, whose deadline is computed from that same `osReadClock`
//     reading (`__Builtins.sleep(ms)`, and so every `sleep(ms)` through `stdlib/Sleep.maxon`'s body).
//   • `__gt_resched` — the cooperative reschedule (`__Builtins.yield()`, and so every `Runtime.yield()`
//     through `stdlib/Runtime.maxon`'s body). It is the same scheduler: a run-queue handoff through
//     `__gt_context_switch`, falling back to `__gt_timer_check`/`__gt_proc_check`, none of which the other
//     three backends provide.
//   • `__cpu_*` — `osCpuCount`'s `GetActiveProcessorCount` (`__Builtins.cpuCount()`). The MACHINE query,
//     and the one whose band-mate is deliberately NOT here: `__sched_max_active_workers` asks the
//     SCHEDULER rather than the OS and its body is a constant return, so it lowers on every target. See
//     `CpuParallelRuntime`'s header for why one file wears two prefixes.
//   • `__thread_*` — `osThreadCpuTicks`'s `QueryThreadCycleTime` (`__Builtins.threadCpuTicks()`). The
//     THREAD-COST query, and `ClockRuntime`'s second band. Its own band-mate `__clock_*` is already here,
//     one bullet up; the two are split by which IMPORT they reach, not by whether they lower.
//
// ⚠ `__gt_io_park` — the filesystem yield — is deliberately ABSENT, and by this set's own rule: SOURCE never
// names it. It is reachable only from the `__mf_*`/`__md_*` bodies, and those are already refused below by
// their prefix tests, so gating it here would only let it be blamed for a call the user did write.
//
// It spans two runtime BANDS — the monotonic clock and the sleep park are `__gt_`-named because the
// SCHEDULER owns that counter and that timer heap (see `ClockRuntime.maxon`'s header) — which is why it is
// one named predicate rather than a prefix test at the call site.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `isGtRuntimeCallee`, AND THAT WIDER TEST WAS MEASURED WRONG.** The `__gt_` band also holds
// calls the compiler INSERTS rather than the user writes — `__gt_promise_drop` is emitted at a SCOPE EXIT
// for an un-awaited promise — and an E3104 against one of those names a runtime entry the user has never
// heard of, at a span where they wrote nothing, blaming the drop instead of the `async`. Measured on the
// wasm suite: two `async-linearity.error.*` cases, whose pinned diagnostic is the target-NEUTRAL
// double-await E3100, additionally reported "'__gt_promise_drop' has no wasm32-wasi implementation" at the
// closing `end`. So a name joins this set when SOURCE names it and the parser routes it here, never because
// it shares a prefix with one that does.
//
// ⚠ **STILL OUTSIDE IT: `async`/`await`.** Their `__gt_*` calls do not exist until lowering — the same
// blind spot `scanRuntimeUsage` documents — so no callee-name test can reach them and gating them honestly
// means gating the async OP KINDS, which is its own piece of work.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SUBPROCESS FAMILY IS NOW IN IT, AND THIS PARAGRAPH RECORDED ITS ABSENCE AS A LIVE DEFECT.** It
// used to read: *"`runProcess(cmd)` and the streaming-subprocess builtins are outside it too: their runtime
// entries (`__gt_process_*`) are not named here, so on another target they still die as a BACKEND PANIC
// rather than a diagnostic — MEASURED"*. All three doors a program can spawn a child through now name their
// entry here — `__Builtins.runProcess` → `__gt_process_run`, `spawnReadLine` → `__gt_io_read`, and the whole
// `__gt_subp_` band behind the seven streaming builtins and the `__Builtins.subprocess*`
// intrinsics (`managedIsNull` sits in that band's parser table but is a heap predicate and lowers nowhere
// near it) —
// so a subprocess program on arm64/linux/wasm is refused at the call's own span instead of panicking three
// tiers down. Each satisfies this set's rule: SOURCE names the builtin and the parser routes it to exactly
// one entry.
//
// ⚠ The band test also admits FOUR entries no source line names — `__gt_subp_yield_read`,
// `__gt_subp_spawn_core`, `__gt_subp_str_from_bytes` and `__gt_subp_argv_cmdline` (count them off the
// `b"__gt_subp_` grep rather than off this sentence; an earlier draft said two). That is harmless HERE, where
// `isGtRuntimeCallee` was measured wrong, and the argument covers all four identically: every one of them is
// reachable only from another `__gt_subp_*` BODY. `checkCalls` walks Maxon-tier ops (user and stdlib source),
// never a hand-built Std graph, so there is no call site for the gate to blame at.
//
// --- the per-family arguments, written where each family joined ---
//
// R4.1's file-IO entries join the clock and sleep ones: `osFileOpenRead` lowers only where a lane has
// written it, and the backends that have not panic rather than fake it — so the program must be refused
// HERE, at the call's own span, before it reaches one.
// R4.3's directory entries join them on the same terms and by the same PREFIX test, so the whole
// eight-method surface is gated by construction rather than by a list somebody has to remember to extend.
// The command-line entries join on the same terms and by the same PREFIX test. ⚠ Their reason is one the
// four above do not have: argv is not merely UNIMPLEMENTED elsewhere, it has a DIFFERENT SHAPE elsewhere
// (one unsplit string on Windows, a pre-split vector under SysV), so serving another target is a rung
// rather than a lowering. `CommandLineRuntime`'s header carries it in full.
// W5's two entries join on the same terms and by the same PREFIX test, and each has a reason of its own
// beyond "unimplemented elsewhere" — the shape argument again, twice over. STANDARD INPUT has no HANDLE
// to fetch under POSIX or WASI (fd 0 *is* the handle; a component holds an input-stream resource), so
// `osStdHandle` has no work to do there. The EXECUTABLE PATH is read by three different OS APIs with three
// different retry protocols (`stdlib/Process.maxon:26-27`), and WASI has no analogue at all.
// `ConsoleRuntime`'s and `ProcessRuntime`'s headers carry each in full.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **FOUR OF THE PARAGRAPHS ABOVE CONCLUDED "A RUNG RATHER THAN A LOWERING", AND MAC4–MAC7 SETTLED
// EACH OF THEM DIFFERENTLY — WHICH IS WHY THE REASONS STAY AND ONLY THE TABLE MOVED.** The `commandLine`
// argument was RIGHT: argv really is a different shape, and arm64-macOS serves it through a different Std
// GRAPH (`CommandLineRuntime.commandLineShape`) rather than a different lowering. The `consoleStdin`
// argument was HALF right: there really is no `GetStdHandle` under POSIX, and it does not follow that a
// rung is needed — an op with no OS work to do lowers to arithmetic, and `osStdHandle` became one
// subtraction. `managedFile` and `managedDirectory` were plain "unimplemented elsewhere", and MAC4/MAC5
// implemented them — the directory half at real cost, because Win32 takes a GLOB where POSIX takes a
// DIRECTORY. ⇒ **A reason on this roster says what a callee NEEDS and never what a target HAS; only
// `targetProvidesFacility` says the second, which is why a landing edits one row and no paragraph.**
// The SUBPROCESS band joins on the same terms and by the same PREFIX test, so an entry point added to it
// later is gated by construction rather than by memory. Its own reason is stronger than "unimplemented
// elsewhere": `CreateProcessA`, three named pipes and an IOCP completion port are a WINDOWS shape, and
// WASI has no process-spawn primitive at all — so serving another target is a rung rather than a
// lowering, and on wasm it is not even that. `__Builtins.runProcess` and `spawnReadLine` are named individually
// because their entries live in the SCHEDULER band rather than behind the subprocess prefix.
// The SOCKET band joins on the same terms and by the same PREFIX test. Its own reason is the
// subprocess band's, one library over: `ws2_32.dll` is a Windows shape, and while WASI does have
// `wasi:sockets`, this slice is not wired to it — so serving another target is a rung rather than a
// lowering.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT WAS MISSING WHEN THE SOCKET RUNTIME LANDED, AND A LEGAL PROGRAM PANICKED THE COMPILER.**
// MEASURED 2026-08-21, `__ManagedSocket.tcpConnect` built with `--target=wasm32-wasi`:
// `panic at StdToWasm.maxon:1634: emitBodyOp: \`osReadClock\` is x64-windows only` — the wasm backend
// tripping over the green-thread park the socket entry opens with, and naming a CLOCK op at a program
// that opened a socket. That is the socket slice's own standing warning made real — ⛔ **the surface
// without the floor is worse than neither**: an earlier attempt declared the names with no runtime behind
// them and turned a clean `E3004` into a backend panic — and it is why this predicate is the FLOOR: every
// non-x64-windows backend panics on these ops BY DESIGN, so the refusal has to happen here, at the
// call's own span, or it does not happen at all.
// The MACHINE query (`__Builtins.cpuCount` ⇒ `__cpu_count`) joins on the same terms and by the same
// PREFIX test. Its own reason is the SHAPE argument again: `GetActiveProcessorCount` and
// `sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)` are two APIs with two return conventions, and WASI has no
// processor-count primitive AT ALL — a component has no OS-thread concept to count. So on that lane the
// refusal is stronger than "not yet": a lowering there could only fabricate a number, and a fabricated
// core count is a silent wrong answer rather than a missing feature.
// ⚠ **ITS BAND-MATE `__sched_max_active_workers` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT**, which is why the two wear
// different prefixes at all: that entry reaches no OS — its whole body is one `.data` load and a `ret`,
// which lowers on every target shv2 emits — so gating it would refuse a load. (It WAS a constant return
// until G1 gave the worker high-water mark a writer; a `.data` load reaches no more OS than a constant.)
// `specs-shv2/builtins-cpu-parallel.md` pins BOTH halves, because the refusal case alone cannot tell a
// live gate from a compiler that refuses the whole family.
// The THREAD-COST query (`__Builtins.threadCpuTicks` ⇒ `__thread_cpu_ticks`) joins on the same terms and
// by the same PREFIX test. Its own reason is the SHAPE argument once more: `QueryThreadCycleTime` answers
// TSC ticks through a `ULONG64*` while `clock_gettime(CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID)` answers NANOSECONDS
// through a `timespec`, so a POSIX lane is a rung rather than a lowering — and WASI has no per-thread CPU
// clock at all. A lowering there could only fabricate a cost, which is the silent wrong answer the
// machine query's paragraph refuses one clause up.
// ⚠ **ITS BAND-MATE, THE SIX `__mm_counter_*` READERS, IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT** for
// `__sched_max_active_workers`'s reason: each is a `.data` load and a `ret`, which lowers on every target
// shv2 emits, and the counters they read are maintained by an allocator that runs on all of them.
// `specs-shv2/builtins-mm-counters.md` pins that acceptance, which is what makes the refusal below a
// NARROW gate rather than a family-wide one.
// ⭐⭐ **THE SELECT (`__Builtins.awaitAny` ⇒ `__gt_await_any`) JOINS INDIVIDUALLY, LIKE ITS THREE
// SCHEDULER BAND-MATES ABOVE (SV3).** It is the whole green-thread substrate — a `TlsAlloc`'d current-P
// slot, a context switch, `WaitForMultipleObjects` — so on any other backend the program dies in a
// deliberate x64-windows gate that knows it is one (`StdToWasm: the P1.5 IOCP ops are x64-windows only`;
// `StdToX64Conversion: lowerTlsAlloc: TlsAlloc is x64-windows only`) rather than at its own span. That is
// exactly the shape `W223` records for the `async`/`await` band, whose entry points are NOT here and
// which is that row's to close; this rung refuses to open ANOTHER such door while it stands, so the new
// intrinsic is named and the E3104 lands at the call.
// MEASURED on `--target=wasm32-wasi`: `error E3104: … it lowers to the runtime entry '__gt_await_any',
// which has no wasm32-wasi implementation`, at the call's own span
// (`specs-shv2/await-any.md`'s `error.rejected-on-wasm`, which reaches its subject through
// `__Builtins.parallelBoundary()` rather than `Runtime.yield()` — the latter is on THIS roster and would
// have masked it four lines earlier).
//
// ⚠ **THE DOOR IS THIS PREDICATE AND NOT `requireTargetSupportsServiceEntry`, which SV1 built for the
// same class one construct over.** That twin exists for an op whose callee is MINTED AT LOWERING and
// which therefore reaches `checkCalls` with no name to key on; `awaitAny` is an ordinary named `call` op
function calleeHostFacility(callee ByteArray) returns CalleeFacility
	// The four scheduler entries named individually: their bands hold calls the compiler inserts, so a
	// prefix test over `__gt_` would blame a drop the user never wrote (measured — see the header above).
	if callee.equals(GtNowNsName) 'monotonic'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.monotonicClock)
	end 'monotonic'

	if callee.equals(GtSleepName) 'sleep'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.sleep)
	end 'sleep'

	if callee.equals(GtReschedName) 'resched'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.reschedule)
	end 'resched'

	if callee.equals(GtAwaitAnyName) 'select'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.greenThreads)
	end 'select'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE PER-P COUNTER SUMS — the select's case exactly, one band over.** Each is a walk of
	// `__sched_procs`, so asking one INSTALLS the whole scheduler (`RuntimeUsage.recordCallUsage` routes all
	// three to `recordGtUsage`), and a lane with no green-thread floor therefore has no TLS slot for the
	// install to reach: it died in a runtime emitter that knows it is x64-windows-only rather than at the
	// call. MEASURED 2026-09-02 on a four-line `__Builtins.schedRetakeCount()` program —
	// `panic at SchedRuntime.maxon:2078: tlsSlotArrayBase: wasi has no per-OS-thread slot this scheduler`
	// (wasm32-wasi) and `panic at StdToX64Conversion.maxon:3719: lowerTlsSetValue: TlsSetValue is
	// x64-windows only` (x64-linux) — which is the `__gt_await_any` paragraph's own argument arriving late:
	// a builtin that turns on `usesGt` and is not named here is a door left open.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ROSTER IS THE ONE `RuntimeUsage` ASKS, NOT A SECOND COPY OF IT.** Both halves of what these
	// three names mean — install the scheduler, require its floor — read `isSchedPerProcSumCallee`, which is
	// why a fourth per-P sum cannot arrive gated at one end and open at the other.
	//
	// ⚠ **THEIR BAND-MATES `__sched_max_active_workers` AND `__sched_processor_count` ARE DELIBERATELY
	// ABSENT**, for the reason the paragraph on the first of them gives below: each is one `.data` load and a
	// `ret`, which lowers on every target shv2 emits, and neither sets `usesGt`.
	// `specs-shv2/builtins-cpu-parallel.md` pins that acceptance, which is what keeps this a NARROW gate
	// rather than a family-wide one.
	if isSchedPerProcSumCallee(callee) 'perProcSums'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.greenThreads)
	end 'perProcSums'

	// The two subprocess surfaces whose entries live in the SCHEDULER band rather than behind the
	// `__gt_subp_` prefix, named individually for that reason.
	if callee.equals(GtProcessRunName) or callee.equals(IoReadName) 'spawnDoors'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.subprocess)
	end 'spawnDoors'

	if isSubprocessRuntimeCallee(callee) 'subprocessBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.subprocess)
	end 'subprocessBand'

	if isSharedMemoryRuntimeCallee(callee) 'sharedMemoryBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.sharedMemory)
	end 'sharedMemoryBand'

	if isClockRuntimeCallee(callee) 'wallClockBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.wallClock)
	end 'wallClockBand'

	if isThreadCpuRuntimeCallee(callee) 'threadCpuBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.threadCpuClock)
	end 'threadCpuBand'

	if isUptimeRuntimeCallee(callee) 'uptimeBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.uptimeClock)
	end 'uptimeBand'

	if isManagedFileRuntimeCallee(callee) 'fileBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.managedFile)
	end 'fileBand'

	if isManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(callee) 'directoryBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.managedDirectory)
	end 'directoryBand'

	if isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(callee) 'socketBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.managedSocket)
	end 'socketBand'

	if isCommandLineRuntimeCallee(callee) 'argvBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.commandLine)
	end 'argvBand'

	if isConsoleRuntimeCallee(callee) 'stdinBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.consoleStdin)
	end 'stdinBand'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE `__tty_` BAND IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THIS FUNCTION, AND ITS FACILITY IS NOT.** It stood
	// here for a day, by the same PREFIX test as its neighbours, and it made this compiler unbuildable for
	// arm64-macOS — see `HostFacility.terminalDetection`, which carries the whole argument. The short of it:
	// this function refuses a call whose facility leaves the program with NO ANSWER, and a missing terminal
	// notion has one. `TerminalRuntime.installTerminalRuntime` reads the facility row instead and picks the
	// BODY, so the callee is present on every target and no lane can go red.

	// The priority WRITE, named individually ahead of its own `__proc_` band for the four scheduler entries'
	// reason one screen up: a prefix band must not silently swallow a member that answers a different
	// facility. It is the one `__proc_` entry that is not a read of an identity.
	if callee.equals(ProcessBackgroundPriorityName) 'priority'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.processPriority)
	end 'priority'

	if isProcessRuntimeCallee(callee) 'processBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.processInfo)
	end 'processBand'

	if isCpuCountRuntimeCallee(callee) 'cpuCountBand'
		return CalleeFacility.needs(HostFacility.cpuCount)
	end 'cpuCountBand'

	return CalleeFacility.none
end 'calleeHostFacility'

// ⭐ **THE TWO QUESTIONS FUSED BACK TOGETHER FOR THE ONE CALLER THAT WANTS THEM THAT WAY** — *"does this
// callee need a facility this target does not provide?"*, which is the E3104 verdict itself.
//
// ⚠ It is a COMPOSITION and not a third fact: both halves come from the two functions above, so a family
// that lands is recorded once, in the table, and this answer follows. The old `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate`
// looked like this from the outside and could not be decomposed from the inside, which is precisely what
// made a partial landing inexpressible.
export function calleeNeedsFacilityTargetLacks(target Target, callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return match calleeHostFacility(callee) 'f'
		needs(facility) gives not targetProvidesFacility(target, facility: facility)
		none gives false
	end 'f'
end 'calleeNeedsFacilityTargetLacks'

// **IS THIS CALLEE ON THE ROSTER AT ALL?** — the TARGET-BLIND half, and the only question
// `StdlibSource.noteSubstrateCallee` may ask: its fixpoint records which entry each stdlib function
// reaches, a property of stdlib source that must not vary with the target being compiled for.
export function calleeHasHostFacility(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return match calleeHostFacility(callee) 'f'
		needs gives true
		none gives false
	end 'f'
end 'calleeHasHostFacility'

// ⭐⭐ **THE CAPABILITY TABLE — what each backend has actually landed.**
//
// Read as *"target T provides facility F"*, and written by the backend that made it true. x64-windows
// provides everything, which is the historical shape: the whole OS substrate was built there first, and the
// old gate's `targetHasWin32Substrate` was this table collapsed to its one full row.
//
// ⚠ **arm64-macOS IS THE FIRST LANE TO GROW THIS TABLE A SECOND COLUMN**, and each `true` in its row is a
// landed family with real libSystem lowerings behind it (`StdToArm64Conversion`, `MachOWriter`'s import
// slots). The rest stay `false` until their lowerings exist — see this file's header on why a premature
// `true` is worse than a `false`.
//
// ⚠ **x64-LINUX HAS ITS OWN BLOCK, AND ONLY TWO ROWS OF IT ARE STILL `false`.** It is a raw-syscall static
// image with no libc, so every family really is a fresh syscall table rather than a re-spelling of the macOS
// work — but the table is x86-64's and the Win32-shaped half of each answer is shared with the arm64 POSIX
// lanes, which is what made argv, the filesystem, the clocks, the green-thread floor and the child-process
// band ports rather than rungs. What remains `false` there is the process PRIORITY and the SOCKETS, each for
// the reason its own row records.
//
// ⚠ **wasm32-wasi's `false`s ARE PERMANENT FOR SOME OF THESE, AND THE TABLE DOES NOT SAY WHICH.** WASI has
// no process-spawn primitive and no per-thread CPU clock at all, so `subprocess` and `threadCpuClock` there
// are refusals of a different KIND from "not yet" — that distinction is carried by
// `SemanticCheck.targetCanHostSubprocess` and its E3074, which reads this table's subject and not its
// answer. One table, two consumers, no second copy.
export function targetProvidesFacility(target Target, facility HostFacility) returns bool
	if target.cpu == CpuArch.x64 and target.os == Os.windows 'theFullRow'
		return true
	end 'theFullRow'

	if target.cpu == CpuArch.arm64 and target.os == Os.macos 'darwinArm64'
		return match facility 'f'
			// MAC3. `mrt_host_monotonic_clock` reads `CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW` through `clock_gettime_nsec_np` and
			// reports the frequency as 1e9, which makes `__gt_now_ns`'s ticks→nanoseconds scaling the identity
			// on this lane (`Arm64DarwinRuntime.DarwinMonotonicTicksPerSecond` carries why that is the strongest
			// form the split can take).
			monotonicClock gives true
			wallClock gives true
			threadCpuClock gives true
			// MAC3. `mrt_host_sleep_ms` is a `nanosleep`; the GREEN-THREAD park above it rides `greenThreads`,
			// which is why the two stay separate rows even though one lane now provides both.
			sleep gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC4/5/6/7.** Each of these four rows was `false` with a REASON attached rather than a
			// deferral, and each reason is now discharged in the file that owned it:
			//
			//   • `managedFile` — `open`/`creat`/`read`/`write`/`close`/`fstat`/`unlink`/`rename`, with the
			//     errno→Win32 translation in `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinLastErrorChunk` so the
			//     target-neutral classification above it is untouched.
			//   • `managedDirectory` — `FindFirstFileA`'s GLOB emulated over `opendir`/`readdir`/`fnmatch`
			//     plus `mkdir` and `getcwd`. It is the row that cost the most, because Win32 takes a pattern
			//     where POSIX takes a directory.
			//   • `consoleStdin` — `ConsoleRuntime`'s own header argued this was "a DIFFERENT Std graph" and
			//     overstated it: `osStdHandle` lowers to one subtraction, and the read is `managedFile`'s.
			//   • `commandLine` — the one row where the SHAPE argument was right: SysV hands a pre-split
			//     vector, so this lane runs a DIFFERENT Std graph over two `.data` words the entry stub
			//     captures (`CommandLineRuntime.commandLineShape`), rather than a different lowering.
			managedFile gives true
			managedDirectory gives true
			commandLine gives true
			consoleStdin gives true
			// MAC2. All three `__proc_` entries lower here: `_NSGetExecutablePath` + `realpath` through the
			// `mrt_host_exe_path` chunk, `getpid`, and `setpriority` + `getpriority`. ⚠ The reason this row
			// took a rung rather than a lowering is intact and now DISCHARGED rather than deferred — the three
			// platforms really do disagree about the path API's shape, and Darwin's disagreement is written out
			// in `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinExePathChunk` instead of being a refusal.
			processInfo gives true
			processPriority gives true
			// `sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)` — the read the op was SHAPED for. `StdOp.osCpuCount` takes no
			// buffer precisely because this lane answers a bare scalar, and the `< 1 ⇒ 1` clamp that covers
			// both `GetActiveProcessorCount`'s 0 and `sysconf`'s -1 is already builder-built Std
			// (`CpuParallelRuntime.buildCpuCount`), so this lane supplies only the read.
			cpuCount gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC3 — THE GREEN-THREAD FLOOR AND EVERYTHING STANDING ON IT.** This row used to read
			// *"`__gt_context_switch`, `__gt_trampoline` and `__gt_morestack` are hand-assembled x64 chunks with
			// no AAPCS64 twin, so a `true` here would promise a scheduler that does not exist"*. They have one
			// now: `Arm64GtRuntime.appendArm64GtRuntimeChunks` builds all three, the prologue stack guard and
			// the import-call stack shim are emitted inline beside them, and the P/M tier's own host objects —
			// the per-thread slot, the run-queue lock, each P's wake event, a worker M's birth and join — are
			// libSystem sequences in `Arm64DarwinRuntime`.
			//
			// ⚠ It is `true` for arm64-macOS ALONE and not for arm64 generally, which is the whole reason this
			// table is keyed on a TARGET rather than on a CPU: every one of those host objects is a pthread
			// primitive, and the Linux lane is a raw static image that links no libc to provide one.
			reschedule gives true
			greenThreads gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **MAC8 — THE LAST FACILITY, AND THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS LANE SELF-COMPILE.** It read *"waits
			// on the scheduler above: a spawn's three pipes are read through `__gt_io_park`"*, and the
			// scheduler is the least of what it needed. Four things landed:
			//
			//   • `posix_spawnp` with a file-actions builder, under `POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT` — which is
			//     `bInheritHandles` + `STARTUPINFOA` exactly, and is why no descriptor is marked by hand.
			//   • A COMMAND SHAPE fork in the Std graph (`SubprocessRuntime.SubprocessCommandShape`): this
			//     primitive takes an ARGUMENT VECTOR, and re-splitting a Win32-quoted line through a shell
			//     would silently expand `$HOME` and glob `a*b`.
			//   • A HANDLE KIND on every value the two handle ops receive
			//     (`Arm64PosixRuntime.PosixHandleKindShift`), because `close` and `free` are not one call
			//     here and a descriptor and a pointer are both just integers.
			//   • An IO-COMPLETION shape (`GtRuntime.IoCompletionShape`): a read completes in its caller on
			//     this lane, so the port, the wake event and the drain thread are not built at all.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE ONE THING IT DOES NOT HAVE IS A NETPOLL A READ CAN PARK ON.** `__gt_subp_read_line`
			// blocks its M until the child writes or exits; the COLLECT loop never blocks, because
			// `osPipePeek` asks first. A kqueue would lift that and change no answer — see
			// `StdToArm64Conversion`'s `osReadOverlapped` arm.
			subprocess gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **THE BSD SOCKET BAND, AND IT IS THE CHEAPEST ROW IN THIS BLOCK — SIX IMPORT SLOTS AND NOT ONE
			// CHUNK.** Winsock and BSD sockets are the same five verbs; `closesocket` is `close` (a socket is a
			// descriptor here), `WSAStartup` has no counterpart at all and lowers to its own answer, and
			// `getaddrinfo`/`freeaddrinfo` are libSystem calls like every other family on this lane — so real DNS,
			// with `/etc/hosts` and the search list, comes with them.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE `sockaddr_in` WINDOWS BUILDS IS NOT THE RECORD THIS LANE WANTS**, because Darwin puts
			// `sin_len` in byte 0 and `sin_family` in byte 1 — see `ManagedSocketRuntime.sockaddrFamilyHalfword`,
			// which carries the measurement and its controls. The Windows spelling is ACCEPTED here by an
			// `AF_UNSPEC` tolerance rather than by being right.
			managedSocket gives true
			// ⭐ **ONE CALL RATHER THAN A DESIGN.**
			// `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinHandleFileTypeChunk` is `isatty` on the descriptor
			// `osStdHandle` already answers, translated into the vocabulary `StdOp.osHandleFileType` speaks
			// (`Arm64PosixRuntime.arm64EmitFileTypeFromStatus`) — this lane's existing errno→Win32 mapping one
			// question over. Conditions 2 and 3 are `getenv`, which the same surface already makes for
			// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`, so the whole facility costs one import slot and one chunk.
			//
			// ⚠ **`isatty` AND NOT THE `ioctl` IT IS BUILT FROM**, because Darwin's `ioctl` is VARIADIC and
			// AAPCS64 passes a variadic argument on the STACK, which a runtime chunk here does not use. A
			// libc-less lane has no such choice — see the arm64-linux row.
			terminalDetection gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **THE NAMED SECTION — `shm_open` + `ftruncate` + `mmap(MAP_SHARED)` + `munmap`, four chunks and
			// three import slots** (`Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s named-shared-section band). It is what carries a
			// DebugStream ring between `maxon monitor` and the child it traces on this lane.
			//
			// ⚠ **`shm_open` IS VARIADIC AND ITS MODE THEREFORE TRAVELS ON THE STACK**, which is the one thing
			// this band needed that no other chunk here does — an outgoing-argument region in a runtime chunk's
			// own frame (`Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction`'s `outgoingArgBytes`).
			sharedMemory gives true
			// `mrt_host_tick_ms` is now gated on the union of its three producers rather than on the pipe band
			// alone (`appendArm64DarwinRuntimeChunks`), so a program that reads the uptime and spawns nothing
			// carries the chunk it branches to.
			uptimeClock gives true
		end 'f'
	end 'darwinArm64'

	if target.cpu == CpuArch.arm64 and target.os == Os.linux 'linuxArm64'
		return match facility 'f'
			// ⭐⭐ **L1 — argv. The FIRST row this lane claims, and it needed no lowering at all.** SysV hands
			// `_start` a pre-split vector on Linux exactly as dyld hands it to macOS in two registers, so
			// `CommandLineRuntime.commandLineShape` already answers `argumentVector` here and the Std graph over
			// the two `.data` words is the one arm64-macOS runs. What was missing was only WHO FILLS THEM: the
			// kernel puts argc at `[sp]` and the vector at `sp+8` and leaves x0/x1 unspecified, so the entry
			// stub's capture is a different four instructions (`Arm64Runtime.emitArm64ArgvFromEntryStack`).
			commandLine gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **L2 — the filesystem, and the console read that rides it.** Every body behind these three is
			// a raw `svc #0` against the AArch64 table in `Arm64LinuxRuntime`, hand-assembled there:
			//
			//   • `managedFile` — `openat`/`read`/`write`/`close`/`fstat`/`unlinkat`/`renameat`, with the
			//     errno→Win32 translation in `Arm64LinuxRuntime.buildArm64LinuxLastErrorChunk`. A raw static
			//     image has no libc and therefore no `errno`, so each chunk STASHES the `-errno` its `svc`
			//     answered into one `.data` word for that translator to read (`LinuxErrnoLabel`).
			//   • `managedDirectory` — `FindFirstFileA`'s GLOB over `openat(O_DIRECTORY)` + `getdents64`, with
			//     the wildcard match HAND-WRITTEN (`mrt_linux_glob_match`): `fnmatch` is a libc function and
			//     this lane links no libc, which is the one place the Darwin surface's shape could not be
			//     reused even in outline.
			//   • `consoleStdin` — `osStdHandle` is one subtraction on every POSIX lane and the read is
			//     `managedFile`'s, exactly as `ConsoleRuntime`'s header says.
			managedFile gives true
			managedDirectory gives true
			consoleStdin gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **L3 — THE TWO NON-SCHEDULER CLOCKS.** `clock_gettime` is a syscall on Linux (113) rather
			// than the `clock_gettime_nsec_np` this backend's macOS arm calls, and it answers a `timespec`
			// rather than a scalar of nanoseconds — so each of these is a frame with two words in it
			// (`Arm64LinuxRuntime.arm64LinuxReadClockTimespec`) rather than a re-spelling of the Darwin body.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **L4 — THE TWO SCHEDULER CLOCKS.** Their old text said they *"stay `false` and are not merely
			// unwritten"* because both are reached through `__gt_now_ns`/`__gt_sleep`, which are SCHEDULER
			// entries — so they land with the green-thread floor below, and they have. `clock_gettime` on
			// `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` reports nanoseconds and a frequency of 1e9, which makes `__gt_now_ns`'s
			// ticks→nanoseconds scaling the identity here as it is on the other lane; the park is
			// `clock_nanosleep` on that SAME clock, which the Darwin lane's `nanosleep` cannot ask for and
			// does not need.
			monotonicClock gives true
			wallClock gives true
			threadCpuClock gives true
			sleep gives true
			// ⭐⭐⭐ **L4 — THE GREEN-THREAD FLOOR. THE ROW ABOVE IT IN THIS TABLE PREDICTED EXACTLY WHAT IT
			// WOULD COST, AND WAS RIGHT.** arm64-macOS's `greenThreads` says this row is `true` "for
			// arm64-macOS ALONE and not for arm64 generally … every one of those host objects is a pthread
			// primitive, and the Linux lane is a raw static image that links no libc to provide one". Each has
			// a `futex` twin now, under the SAME `mrt_host_*` symbol:
			//
			//   • THE WAKE EVENT is a single word rather than a mutex + condvar + signal word. `FUTEX_WAIT`
			//     compares and sleeps INSIDE the kernel, so the mutex a condvar needs to make its wait atomic
			//     is not needed at all — which is what removes four `_init`/`_destroy` calls and 112 bytes of
			//     opaque struct from every object.
			//   • THE RUN-QUEUE LOCK is a three-word RECURSIVE futex mutex. Recursion is not optional and was
			//     measured as a hang on the other lane (`__slab_refill` taking the slab's own lock twice).
			//   • THE PER-OS-THREAD SLOT is `TPIDR_EL0` pointing at an `mmap`'d page. The absence of a libc is
			//     what MAKES that available rather than what blocks it: nothing else on a static image reads or
			//     writes the writable thread pointer.
			//
			//   • **A WORKER M IS A RAW `clone`**, and it is the last of the five to be built. This paragraph
			//     read *"one of them is NOT built: `osThreadCreate` IS A RUNTIME ABORT HERE … so a `clone`
			//     would be untested dead code"*, on the measurement that neither Std-tier producer could reach
			//     the op. `spawn` publishes a GREEN thread, the publish doors' green arms call
			//     `__sched_wake_or_spawn`, and the default processor count is the machine's — so the second
			//     producer came back and the abort said so by name.
			//     `Arm64LinuxRuntime.buildArm64LinuxThreadCreateChunk` is that `clone`: its own stack with a
			//     `PROT_NONE` guard page, its own slot block through `CLONE_SETTLS`, and a
			//     `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` word the joiner sleeps on.
			//
			// ⇒ **THIS LANE IS MULTI-M EXACTLY AS arm64-macOS IS**, which is what makes `StdDialect`'s
			// barrier-free `completed` publication a live question on BOTH weakly-ordered lanes rather than a
			// latent one on either (that note carries the shape of the fix it is owed). A `true` in this row
			// promises the isel lowers every `StdOp` the facility's bodies emit, and it does.
			reschedule gives true
			greenThreads gives true
			// ⭐⭐⭐ **L5 — THE LAST FACILITY, AND THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS LANE SELF-COMPILE.** Its old text read
			// *"waits on the scheduler above, as it does on every lane: a spawn's three pipes are read through
			// the green-thread park"*, and L4 discharged that half. What was left was the spawn itself, and
			// ⛔ **LINUX HAS NO SPAWN PRIMITIVE TO CALL**: `posix_spawn` is a libc FUNCTION, not a syscall, and
			// AArch64 dropped the legacy spelling of everything a hand-built one needs — no `fork`, no `pipe`,
			// no `dup2`, no `poll`. So the child is `clone(SIGCHLD)`, `pipe2`, `dup3`, `ppoll` and `execve`,
			// hand-assembled in `Arm64LinuxRuntime.buildArm64LinuxSpawnChunk`.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **THE REST OF THE BAND IS ONE BODY FOR BOTH POSIX LANES, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF HOW EVERY
			// OTHER FAMILY IN THIS TABLE SPLIT.** `waitpid` and `wait4` decode the same status word, `poll` and
			// `ppoll` fill the same `struct pollfd`, and the reap, the bounded await, the exit-code read and the
			// netpoll's multi-wait make no OS call at all — so they live in `Arm64PosixRuntime` and take this
			// lane's `svc` through `PosixHostCall` (see that file's L5 header).
			//
			// ⚠ **A FAILED EXEC IS REPORTED THROUGH A `O_CLOEXEC` STATUS PIPE**, because a hand-built fork has
			// no other channel: `posix_spawnp` answers its caller, while here the failure happens in another
			// process. Without it the Std tier's `hProcess == 0` — the whole of `SubprocessError.spawnFailed` —
			// would be unreachable on this lane.
			subprocess gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **L3 — the process's IDENTITY, and the row whose old text said outright that it was "three
			// separate ports rather than one".** Two of the three are done: `getpid` is one `svc` and the
			// executable path is `readlinkat` on `/proc/self/exe` through `mrt_host_exe_path`, because
			// `_NSGetExecutablePath` has no Linux counterpart at all.
			processInfo gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **L4 — THE THIRD, AND ITS OBJECTION IS NOW ANSWERED BY INHERITANCE ALONE.** It read:
			// *"`setpriority` is a WRITE whose scope disagrees: POSIX scopes a nice value to the THREAD on Linux
			// and to the PROCESS on Darwin, so this lane owes thread-inheritance work at `StdOp.osThreadCreate`
			// before a `true` here would be honest."* L4 answered it with *"there are no other threads on this
			// lane"*, which the worker-M `clone` has falsified — so what is left carrying the row is the
			// objection's OTHER half, which is real: `clone` inherits its creator's nice value at birth, so
			// every M started AFTER the call runs at the priority the call asked for.
			//
			// ⛔ **WHAT IS STILL OWED, STATED RATHER THAN CLAIMED DISCHARGED: an M that already existed WHEN
			// `__proc_bg_priority` ran keeps the default nice.** Windows and Darwin lower the whole PROCESS in
			// one call and have nothing to walk; here the calls would have to reach every live M's tid, which
			// means a tid in the P struct and a walk in a chunk rather than the two inline `svc`s
			// `lowerOsEnterBackgroundPriorityLinux` emits. No case reaches it — the specs read the value back
			// on the calling thread, which is exactly the thread the call did scope to — so it is recorded
			// here as the gap it is.
			//
			// ⚠ The lowering also folds `getpriority`'s SYSCALL BIAS: the raw call answers `20 - nice`, and
			// without the fold `answers-the-posix-nice-value` would pass by the coincidence that `20 - 10` is
			// 10 while every other nice value read back wrong (MEASURED on an aarch64 guest — nice 15 answered
			// 5). See `LinuxRuntime.LinuxGetPriorityBias`.
			processPriority gives true
			// ⭐ **L3 — `sched_getaffinity` and a popcount, which is what the old text here already named.** It
			// is preferred to reading `/sys/devices/system/cpu/online` for two reasons: it needs no filesystem
			// (this lane's file surface is a whole other facility, and a machine query must not depend on one),
			// and it reports the CPUs THIS PROCESS MAY ACTUALLY USE rather than the CPUs the machine has —
			// which is what a parallelism hint wants, and what `nproc` itself answers.
			cpuCount gives true
			// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE SOCKET BAND — FIVE INLINE `svc`s AND A RESOLVER WRITTEN IN Std IR, BECAUSE THERE IS NO
			// `getaddrinfo` ON A LANE THAT LINKS NO LIBC.** The transfers are `sendto`/`recvfrom` with a NULL peer
			// (AArch64 has no `send`/`recv` syscall at all) and every raw answer is folded from `-errno` to the
			// exact -1 the Std tier tests for (`StdToArm64Conversion.emitSyscallFailureSentinel`).
			//
			// ⭐ **THE RESOLVE IS A DIFFERENT Std GRAPH RATHER THAN A DIFFERENT LOWERING** — the answer
			// `commandLineShape` gives for argv, one family over. `ManagedSocketRuntime`'s
			// `SocketResolveShape.builtinResolver` parses a numeric address, consults `/etc/hosts`, then asks the
			// first `nameserver` in `/etc/resolv.conf` over TCP, all in Std IR — so ONE body serves both Linux
			// lanes and the isel that already lowers every op it uses compiles it.
			//
			// ⚠ **WHAT THAT RESOLVER DOES NOT DO IS STATED AT ITS OWN HEADER AND IS NOT HIDDEN BY THIS `true`**:
			// `A` records only, the first nameserver only, no `search`/`domain` suffixing, no CNAME chasing and NO
			// TIMEOUT. A `true` here promises that every `StdOp` the facility's bodies emit is lowered, and it is;
			// it does not promise `getaddrinfo`'s reach.
			//
			// ⚠ **IT MAKES THE FILE SURFACE A DEPENDENCY OF THE SOCKET ONE ON THIS LANE**, which is why
			// `LinuxRuntime.linuxOpensFiles` names the socket bit: a socket program with no `File` call in it still
			// lays out `mrt_host_file_open`.
			managedSocket gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **THE TERMINAL TEST IS THE `ioctl` `isatty` IS MADE OF, BECAUSE THIS LANE LINKS NO LIBC TO
			// ASK.** `ioctl(fd, TCGETS, &termios)` succeeds exactly on a terminal; `ioctl` is syscall **29** on
			// AArch64 and `TCGETS` is **0x5401** (`LinuxRuntime`), and the kernel writes **36** bytes, so
			// `buildArm64LinuxHandleFileTypeChunk` is a chunk with a frame rather than an inline `svc`.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **AND CONDITIONS 2 AND 3 ARE WHAT THIS ROW REALLY COST.** `NO_COLOR` and `TERM` make the
			// detecting body emit `StdOp.osEnvRead`, which on a libc-less lane walks `__mrt_envp` — a `.data`
			// word only the ENTRY STUB can fill. All three gates on that capture ride
			// `PosixRuntime.posixReadsEnvironment` rather than the scheduler's bit, because a terminal-detecting
			// program need not have a scheduler; gated on `usesGt` alone the first such program relocated
			// against a word nothing wrote.
			terminalDetection gives true
			// The two host calls behind `__shm_*` are Win32 section calls with no lowering on this lane.
			sharedMemory gives false
			// `mrt_host_tick_ms` exists on this lane and is installed only under `usesIocp`
			// (see `HostFacility.uptimeClock`), so a program that reads the uptime and spawns nothing would
			// relocate against a chunk the image does not carry.
			uptimeClock gives false
		end 'f'
	end 'linuxArm64'

	if target.cpu == CpuArch.x64 and target.os == Os.linux 'linuxX64'
		return match facility 'f'
			// ⭐⭐ **argv — the row this lane claims with no lowering at all**, exactly as arm64-linux claims it.
			// SysV hands the kernel's pre-split vector to `_start` on the STACK, so
			// `CommandLineRuntime.commandLineShape` already answers `argumentVector` here and the Std graph over
			// the two `.data` words is the one both other lanes run. What was missing was only WHO FILLS THEM:
			// argc at `[rsp]` and the vector at `rsp+8`, captured by the frameless entry stub
			// (`BackendDispatch.appendLinuxArgvCapture`).
			commandLine gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **THE FILESYSTEM, AND THE CONSOLE READ THAT RIDES IT.** Every body behind these three is a
			// raw `syscall` against the x86-64 table in `X64LinuxRuntime`, hand-assembled there:
			//
			//   • `managedFile` — `openat`/`read`/`write`/`close`/`fstat`/`unlinkat`/`renameat`, with the
			//     errno→Win32 translation reading a `.data` word each body stashes into
			//     (`X64LinuxErrnoLabel`), because a raw static image has no libc and therefore no `errno`.
			//   • `managedDirectory` — `FindFirstFileA`'s GLOB over `openat(O_DIRECTORY)` + `getdents64`,
			//     with the wildcard match HAND-WRITTEN (`mrt_linux_glob_match`): `fnmatch` is a libc
			//     function and this lane links none.
			//   • `consoleStdin` — `osStdHandle` is one subtraction on every POSIX lane and the read is
			//     `managedFile`'s, exactly as `ConsoleRuntime`'s header says.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE Win32-SHAPED HALF OF ALL THREE IS `Arm64PosixRuntime`'s AND IS READ FROM THERE** —
			// the `dwFileAttributes` a `st_mode` becomes, the FILETIME a whole second becomes, the errno
			// mapping and the glob split are facts neither OS nor ISA has a say in. What is genuinely x64's
			// is the syscall table, the register ABI and `struct stat`'s layout.
			managedFile gives true
			managedDirectory gives true
			consoleStdin gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO NON-SCHEDULER CLOCKS.** `clock_gettime` is syscall 228 here (113 on the other
			// Linux lane) and answers a `timespec` rather than a scalar of nanoseconds — so each is a frame
			// with two words in it (`X64LinuxRuntime.x64LinuxReadClockTimespec`) rather than a re-spelling
			// of any other lane's body. `CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID` reports NANOSECONDS where Windows reports
			// TSC ticks, which `__thread_cpu_ticks`' own contract permits: it promises only that two
			// readings may be SUBTRACTED.
			wallClock gives true
			threadCpuClock gives true
			// ⭐ **THE IDENTITY: `getpid` is one inline `syscall` (39) and the executable path is
			// `readlinkat` on `/proc/self/exe`**, because `GetModuleFileNameA` has no Linux counterpart and
			// no call answers the question — the kernel publishes a SYMLINK instead.
			processInfo gives true
			// ⭐ **`sched_getaffinity` AND A POPCOUNT**, preferred to reading
			// `/sys/devices/system/cpu/online` for two reasons: it needs no filesystem (a machine query must
			// not drag in a whole other facility), and it reports the CPUs THIS PROCESS MAY USE rather than
			// the CPUs the machine has — which is what a parallelism hint wants and what `nproc` answers.
			cpuCount gives true
			// ⛔⛔ **THESE FIVE ROWS ARE INDIVISIBLE: A LANE PROVIDES ALL OF THEM OR NONE, AND FLIPPING ONE
			// ALONE MAKES A PROGRAM THAT PANICS IN THE ISEL.** Whoever next touches them is bound by it:
			//
			//   * `monotonicClock` and `sleep` are reached ONLY through `__gt_now_ns`/`__gt_sleep`, which are
			//     SCHEDULER entries — so neither means anything without `greenThreads`.
			//   * `reschedule` is `__gt_context_switch`, a hand-assembled per-CPU chunk of the same floor.
			//   * `subprocess` cannot lag behind `greenThreads` EITHER, because `installGtRuntime` calls
			//     `buildGtProcessRun` UNCONDITIONALLY (`GtRuntime.maxon`): every green-thread program
			//     statically contains `__gt_process_run`, whose `osProcessSpawn`/`osWaitHandles`/
			//     `osProcessExitCode` reach a lowering that must have this lane's arm. A `greenThreads` row
			//     set with `subprocess` false compiles no `async` program at all — it panics in the isel on a
			//     spawn no source in the program mentions.
			//
			// ⚠ **MEASURED, not inferred from the call graph.** The smallest possible `async` program — one
			// `async`, one `await`, one `sleep` to make the callee yield — compiled for x64-WINDOWS, the lane
			// where every one of these lowerings exists, binds imports including `CreateProcessA`,
			// `GetQueuedCompletionStatus` AND `CreateThread`. Dead-function elimination does not remove the
			// child-process band from a program that spawns nothing.
			monotonicClock gives true
			sleep gives true
			reschedule gives true
			greenThreads gives true
			subprocess gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **THE arm64-linux ROW'S TWO SYSCALLS WITH THIS LANE'S NUMBERS, AND THE NUMBERS ARE SWAPPED
			// RATHER THAN MERELY DIFFERENT.** x86-64 has `getpriority` at 140 and `setpriority` at 141;
			// AArch64 takes `asm-generic/unistd.h`'s order and has them the other way round, so a lane reusing
			// the twin's constant would SET when it meant to READ. Both were VERIFIED by invoking them on an
			// x86-64 guest — see `X64LinuxRuntime.X64LinuxSyscallGetPriority`.
			//
			// ⛔ **THE SAME GAP THE arm64-linux ROW RECORDS, STATED RATHER THAN CLAIMED DISCHARGED: an M that
			// already existed when `__proc_bg_priority` ran keeps the default nice.** Linux scopes a nice
			// value to the calling THREAD, and `clone` inherits its creator's at birth — so every M started
			// AFTER the call runs at the priority asked for and only an earlier one does not. Closing it needs
			// each M's tid in its P and a walk in a chunk, where this is two inline `syscall`s. No case reaches
			// it: the specs read the value back on the calling thread, which is the thread the call scoped to.
			//
			// ⚠ The lowering also folds `getpriority`'s SYSCALL BIAS — the raw call answers `20 - nice`
			// (`LinuxRuntime.LinuxGetPriorityBias`), and without the fold `answers-the-posix-nice-value` would
			// pass by the coincidence that `20 - 10` is 10 while every other value read back wrong.
			processPriority gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **THE SOCKET BAND — the arm64-linux row's lowerings with this lane's own syscall numbers
			// (41/42/44/45 against 198/203/206/207) and its own register ABI, and the SAME Std-IR resolver.** That
			// resolver is why the split between the two Linux lanes here is four numbers rather than a second
			// implementation: it is a Std graph, so the isel that already lowers every op it uses compiles it
			// unchanged. Its reach — and what it does not reach — is stated at `ManagedSocketRuntime`'s resolver
			// header and summarised in the arm64-linux row.
			managedSocket gives true
			// ⭐⭐ **THE SAME `ioctl(fd, TCGETS, &termios)` AS THE OTHER LINUX LANE, WITH THIS ONE'S OWN NUMBER.**
			// `ioctl` is **16** on x86-64 where it is 29 on AArch64; `TCGETS` (0x5401) and the **36** bytes the
			// kernel writes are shared, and live in `LinuxRuntime` for that reason.
			//
			// ⭐⭐ **AND THE WHOLE ENV-READ FACILITY LANDED WITH IT, BECAUSE CONDITIONS 2 AND 3 ARE NOT
			// OPTIONAL.** This lane had no part of `StdOp.osEnvRead`: the isel now routes it to
			// `buildX64LinuxEnvReadChunk` on the syscall route, and `BackendDispatch.appendLinuxEnvpCapture`
			// stashes the vector the kernel left on the stack. ⭐ The env-read gate is
			// `PosixRuntime.posixReadsEnvironment` — a union whose OTHER producer, the scheduler's
			// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` read, cannot fire here yet — so the day `greenThreads` lands it needs no widening.
			terminalDetection gives true
			// The two host calls behind `__shm_*` are Win32 section calls with no lowering on this lane.
			sharedMemory gives false
			// `mrt_host_tick_ms` exists on this lane and is installed only under `usesIocp`
			// (see `HostFacility.uptimeClock`), so a program that reads the uptime and spawns nothing would
			// relocate against a chunk the image does not carry.
			uptimeClock gives false
		end 'f'
	end 'linuxX64'

	return false
end 'targetProvidesFacility'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS TARGET PROVIDE EVERY FACILITY?** — the per-FUNCTION short-circuit
// `SemanticCheck.checkCalls` settles once and hands to every call op, in place of the old
// `targetHasWin32Substrate`. Written as a walk over `allCases` rather than as `x64 and windows` so that the
// day a second lane completes the table it becomes true here by MEASUREMENT rather than by someone
// remembering to widen a hard-coded pair.
// ⭐⭐ **CAN THIS TARGET CARRY THE DebugStream PRODUCER? — the ONE producer of that fact.** Two parties ask
// it and they are in different trees: `Main.runBuild` refuses `--debugstream` at the command line, and the
// spec harness skips a capture case rather than compiling one. Spelled at each, it was a target pair written
// twice beside the table that already held the answer.
//
// It is a CONJUNCTION of facilities rather than a list of targets because that is what `__ds_init` actually
// needs: it attaches to a named shared section, and every entry it writes stamps the millisecond stopwatch.
// A lane that gains both gains the producer here by MEASUREMENT.
export function targetCanProduceDebugStream(target Target) returns bool
	return targetProvidesFacility(target, facility: HostFacility.sharedMemory) and targetProvidesFacility(target, facility: HostFacility.uptimeClock)
end 'targetCanProduceDebugStream'

export function targetProvidesEveryFacility(target Target) returns bool
	for facility in HostFacility.allCases 'each'
		if not targetProvidesFacility(target, facility: facility) 'missing'
			return false
		end 'missing'
	end 'each'

	return true
end 'targetProvidesEveryFacility'
